# Users, Groups, and Permissions

Every file has an owner, a group, and permissions for the owner, group, and
other users. The permission letters are `r` (read), `w` (write), and `x`
(execute).

Run `ls -l` to view permissions, for example:

```text
-rw-r--r-- 1 sam staff 120 Oct 31  notes.txt
```

Here, the owner can read and write; the group and others can read. Use
`chmod` to change permissions and `chown` to change ownership. Some
administrative operations require `sudo`, which runs a command with elevated
privileges; use it only when needed and only with commands you understand.
